[metadata]
title: How to Get Perfect Lighting for YouTube Videos (Beginner Guide)
channel: @octopusinc_
duration: 13:41
source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=aII5z6Bkl5g
upload_date: 20260715
transcript_source: captions+frames
route: visual

---

[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Host introduces the topic of lighting for YouTube videos, showing a Sony A7S III camera.
AUDIO: 'Lighting is one of the best ways to make your videos look more professional. In fact, most beginners make the mistake of buying an expensive camera thinking it\'s going to make their shot look better when in reality they just need better lighting. This was shot on the iPhone 15'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and I think it looks a lot better than my Sony A7S3 with bad light and this thing cost me a lot of cash. So, for the rest of this video, I\'m going to ditch this expensive piece of metal and shoot'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'everything on the iPhone. In this video, you\'ll not only learn how to get perfect lighting for your YouTube videos, but you\'ll also learn three different lighting setups for three different budgets. And I\'ll share my'

[00:38] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: Host speaking directly to the camera.
AUDIO: 'favorite beginner lights for each budget range. But don\'t skip ahead because even if you buy the most expensive light, your shot could still look like this. That\'s because before you buy any light,'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you need to understand the difference between hard light and soft light. This is a $300 light, but the lighting looks really harsh and aggressive on my face. And this is a $40 light that looks much'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'softer. The $40 light looks so much better because it\'s utilizing diffusion, which spreads the light out and creates softer looking shadows. We\'re going to break diffusion down into two steps,'

[01:14] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Animated graphic showing light diffusion.
AUDIO: 'size and distance. On the left, I\'m shining a flashlight directly at my face. Notice how aggressive the shadows are. On the right, I\'m using the same flashlight, except this time I\'m passing'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it through a white bed sheet. Notice how much softer the light looks on my face. That\'s because the bed sheet is acting as a diffusion layer between the light in my face. As the light passes through the sheet, it spreads out and'

[01:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'effectively becomes larger, which creates softer looking shadows. If you go outside and it\'s cloudy, you might notice that the light looks a lot softer on your face than when it\'s sunny out. That\'s because the clouds are'

[01:53] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Host demonstrating the effect of a harsh light source (Neewer light) on his face.
AUDIO: 'essentially acting as Earth\'s bed sheet. Without the clouds, notice how small the sun appears in the sky, kind of like a flashlight. But when you add the clouds, notice how that light gets spread out across the sky. So step number one to'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'make our light softer is to increase the size of the light. Now the second step is to put the light closer to our face. Ideally, we want to make the light as big as possible, and we want to place it'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'as close to the subject\'s face without getting the light in the shot. If the light is further away, it appears smaller relative to the subject. When the light source is smaller, the light hits your face from a more specific'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'direction, creating darker shadows and more contrast. When we move the light closer, it effectively becomes a larger light source and softens the shadows because the light wraps around the'

[02:43] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Host speaking to the camera with warm, directional lighting.
AUDIO: 'features on your face, creating a much smoother and more flattering look. If you\'ve ever seen behindthescenes footage from a professional photo or video shoot, notice how big the lights are and how close they are to the subject\'s'

[02:56] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'face. Now, just because you have soft light does not guarantee perfect lighting. you could still end up with shots that look like this, this, and this. That\'s because there are three massive beginner mistakes that need to'

[03:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'be avoided at all costs. Mistake number one, using ceiling lights. Now, I know it can be tempting, especially if you don\'t have any film making lights. But these lights create two massive problems for us. Number one, they create huge'

[03:24] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Host speaking to the camera, with an on-screen graphic showing "wb" (white balance) and a slider.
AUDIO: 'shadows under your eyes because the light is coming straight down from the ceiling, which doesn\'t look very cinematic. It just kind of puts this raccoon eye look on your face. Raccoons are cute, though. And number two, if we'

[03:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'have the ceiling lights on and we\'re filming with a film makingaking light like this, it often causes huge problems with the white balance on our camera. This is because most ceiling lights have a different color temperature than our'

[03:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'filming light. When our face is being lit by two different color temperatures, our camera has a difficult time trying to figure out what true white should be. This mix and color temperature results in skin tones that don\'t look that'

[04:03] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Host speaking to the camera, giving a thumbs up.
AUDIO: 'natural. For example, in this shot, the ceiling lights are on and in this shot they are off. This shot looks way more cinematic. So to get professional looking lighting, we have to turn off'

[04:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'all of the lights in our room. Block off any extra light coming in through windows and use one main light source. That gives your camera one consistent color to work with and your skin tones'

[04:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'will look a lot more natural. Mistake number two, maxing out your light intensity. In this shot, I turn the brightness all the way up to 100. Notice how dark the background gets. That\'s because there\'s so much light hitting my'

[04:40] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Host speaking to the camera, demonstrating uneven lighting with blue and yellow hues.
AUDIO: 'face that my camera has to darken the entire image. So, my face is not overexposed. Since the background isn\'t getting nearly as much light as my face, it ends up looking a lot darker. Now,'

[04:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'watch what happens when I turn the brightness of my light down. My face isn\'t nearly as bright anymore, so the camera doesn\'t have to darken the image as much. As a result, the background'

[05:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'looks a lot brighter. So, the brighter your subject is compared to the background, the darker the background will appear. Mistake number three, lighting both sides of your face evenly. Notice how this shot does not look as'

[05:19] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: Overhead shot of the host sitting in a chair with a ring light around him.
AUDIO: 'good as this shot. The reason is because I placed the light directly in front of my face. This removes almost all of the shadows on my face and doesn\'t really look that professional. Ends up looking'

[05:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more like a Zoom meeting. For YouTube videos, especially if we want that cinematic style, we actually want some shadows. This is too dramatic. This looks like a webcam. And this is a good'

[05:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'balance right in the middle. To get this look, all I did was move the light to about a 45° angle in front of me. Also, real quick, while we\'re on this topic, if you want cinematic lighting, I would'

[05:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'definitely avoid getting a circular ring light. I know they\'re convenient because they work as both a light and a tripod, but if you\'re trying to make your videos look more professional, they end up removing the shadows on your face. Like'

[06:07] [scene:setup]
VISUAL: Host opening curtains to demonstrate natural window light.
AUDIO: 'we just talked about, it doesn\'t really look that cinematic. All right, let\'s look at three easy lighting setups that you can do. And the first one is great because it doesn\'t have to cost you anything. If you\'re just getting started'

[06:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and you don\'t have any lights yet, using a window as your light source is one of the fastest ways to make your shots look more professional. What we want to do is position the window in front of the subject\'s face. Now, again, we don\'t'

[06:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'want the subject facing the window perfectly straight on because that will light both sides of their face evenly. Instead, we want to position them slightly to the side so they\'re looking toward the window at an angle. This will'

[06:44] [scene:setup]
VISUAL: A softbox light setup next to a window.
AUDIO: 'create a nice soft shadow on one side of their face. Now, depending on the weather, you might have soft light or hard light. So, if it\'s sunny out, you can hang up a bed sheet in front of your window to help diffuse the light. That'

[06:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'should make it look a lot softer. All right, moving on to the second setup, the softbox. The softbox basically takes the sun and puts it on a light stand. And instead of using a bed sheet for diffusion, it uses a diffusion screen.'

[07:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Now, the main benefit of using a softbox instead of the sun is now we have control over the intensity and direction of the light. My favorite direction to point the softbox is at a 45 degree'

[07:22] [scene:setup]
VISUAL: Host sitting between two softbox lights, demonstrating a two-light setup.
AUDIO: 'angle in front of the subject. And as far as intensity goes, I like to keep it fairly low so the background doesn\'t get super dark. Now, if there\'s too much shadow on one side of the face, you can'

[07:34] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'move the light a little bit closer to center. Again, we just want to avoid putting it directly in front of the subject\'s face. All right, moving on to the third setup, and what I like to call the pro setup. And by pro, I don\'t mean it has to cost you hundreds of dollars.'

[07:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'This setup only costs 80 bucks. The main difference with this setup is that we\'re adding accent lighting and another softbox. With the first softbox setup, we positioned one softbox about 45° in'

[08:02] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Close-up of the host's face with arrows indicating light direction.
AUDIO: 'front of the subject. And while that looks really good, sometimes the shadow on the other side of the face can be a little too dark. So with this setup, we\'re going to add a second softbox on'

[08:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the opposite side and set it to roughly half the brightness of our main softbox. In film making terms, the main light is called the key light and the second light is called the fill light. The reason we don\'t want the fill light at'

[08:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the same intensity as the key light. It\'s because it\'s going to flatten out the face and remove those nice cinematic shadows. The fill light\'s job is just to fill in some of that shadow, but not to get rid of it completely. If you want'

[08:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'more shadow, you can turn the brightness down on the fill light. And if you want less shadow, you can turn the brightness up on the fill light. Totally personal preference. Just depends on the style that you\'re going for. Now, the second'

[08:52] [scene:explanation]
VISUAL: Host speaking to the camera with balanced lighting.
AUDIO: 'component of this lighting setup is accent lighting. This can be done using practical lights around your house or you can pick up some small colorchanging tubes like these. I like to place these in the background of the shot. Try to'

[09:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'balance out the light so one side of the frame isn\'t overloaded with light. For example, in this shot, notice how all of the accent light is on this side. It looks good, but I think this shot works better because the lighting is more'

[09:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'balanced. All right, so now that you know what to do with the lights, you might be wondering what lights should you buy. I\'m going to break my recommendations down into three different budgets: $50, $100, and $300. For a $50 budget, I recommend the Mount'

[09:34] [scene:recommendations]
VISUAL: Host speaking to the camera, demonstrating lighting with a warm tone.
AUDIO: 'Dog softbox light. The light comes with a 19inx 27 in softbox, which is a really good size for the price. This light also has the ability to adjust the color'

[09:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'temperature, which can make your footage look more cool or more warm. If you\'re just getting started, this light will level up your content a ton. For the $100 budget, I would recommend the two light mount dog softbox lighting kit'

[09:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'along with two newer light wands. This setup allows you to use one softbox as your key light and the other one as your fill light. Then you can use the two light wands as accent lights in the'

[10:11] [scene:recommendations]
VISUAL: A Neewer MS150C monolite with a softbox.
AUDIO: 'background of your shot. For example, you can put one wand on each side of the background to help balance out the frame. Or if you already have a practical light in your room, like a lamp, you can place that on one side and then place the light wands on the other'

[10:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'side. This is a great setup if you\'re looking to level up your videos without breaking the bank. And for our $300 budget, I would recommend the newer MS150C monolite paired with the newer 45cm'

[10:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'softbox. If you have the budget, this light is a great option. It has a 150W COB light, which means it can get a lot brighter than the Mount Dog setup. It'

[10:48] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Host speaking to the camera, smiling.
AUDIO: 'also has a Bowen\'s mount, which allows you to put a bunch of different modifiers like lanterns, octaboxes, and grids on the front of the light. One of my favorite modifiers is attaching a grid to the front of the softbox. A grid'

[11:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is used to have more control over where the light goes. Without a grid, the light kind of spreads everywhere, but when you use a grid, it keeps the light more focused on your subject. If you pair this light and grid setup with two'

[11:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'newer light tubes, you can get a pretty epic looking shot. This is one of those investment lights that you\'re probably not going to outgrow. there\'s a good chance that you\'ll still be using it three to five years from now. Now, as'

[11:27] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Fast-paced montage of video editing software and other content creation elements.
AUDIO: 'you\'ve seen, lighting has a huge impact on your shot, but lighting is just one piece of the puzzle when it comes to content creation. For example, these two shots are lit exactly the same, but they look completely different because I'

[11:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'messed up the settings on this shot. And the truth is, putting all of these puzzle pieces together takes a very long time. And it\'s also just pretty overwhelming. One day you\'re watching a video about cameras, the next day you\'re'

[11:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'learning about lighting, then editing, then audio. Before you know it, you\'re stuck in tutorial prison. That\'s exactly why we created 14-day Filmmaker. It\'s our ultra affordable master class designed to give you a clear road map'

[12:08] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Promotional graphic for "14 Day Smartphone Filmmaker" course with "LIFETIME ACCESS" text.
AUDIO: 'from complete beginner to pro in the fastest time frame possible. Not only will you learn about the best gear, settings, lighting, camera movements, storytelling, editing, and all of the'

[12:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'other technical skills, but we also give you practice exercises so you can turn theory into real knowledge. We also give you downloadable practice footage to follow along with, editing templates,'

[12:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and PDF guides. You also get lifetime access to our private student community where Anthony, the mastermind behind the course, personally hosts a weekly live Q&amp;A call. So, you can jump on and ask'

[12:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'him any questions you want. And if you sign up using the link in the description below, you\'ll actually get lifetime access to three courses in total. 14-day smartphone filmmaker, 14-day filmmaker Pro Camera Edition,'

[12:57] [scene:outro]
VISUAL: Another host speaking to the camera, with a desk setup in the background.
AUDIO: 'which covers everything you need to know if you do choose to upgrade to larger cameras down the road. And finally, you also get one week director, which is a crash course on Hollywood level storytelling and directing, even if you'

[13:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'don\'t have a budget. Over 200,000 people from all over the world have enrolled. And here\'s just one before and after a student posted in our community. All they used was their smartphone. Pretty epic. If you want to get lifetime access'

[13:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and level up your content, the link to enroll is in the description below. But other than that, thank you so much for watching. Hit the subscribe button and I\'ll see you in the next one.'
